From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: server, ntdll, kernelbase: restricted and AppContainer tokens, integrity levels

Chromium's sandbox (Microsoft Edge, Chrome) runs its child processes with
restricted tokens at a lowered integrity level, and Edge runs its renderers
in an AppContainer. Wine had none of it: NtFilterToken ignored restricting
SIDs and its flags, the user SID could not be made deny-only,
TokenRestrictedSids was unhandled (so IsTokenRestricted failed), setting
TokenIntegrityLevel was a stub and querying it always answered "high",
NtCreateLowBoxToken returned a NULL token, and kernelbase had no
CreateAppContainerToken -- Edge looked it up, found nothing, and silently
never started a renderer: the tab stayed blank.

Restricted tokens
- A token keeps its restricting SIDs. An access check on a restricted token
  must be granted twice, once by the token's own SIDs and once by the
  restricting SIDs; a write-restricted token (WRITE_RESTRICTED) is checked
  twice only for write access (not READ_CONTROL or SYNCHRONIZE, which the
  generic write mapping also names). Duplicates stay restricted;
  restricting an already-restricted token keeps only the SIDs both lists
  name.
- Disabling the user SID makes it deny-only: it then matches deny ACEs only.
- DISABLE_MAX_PRIVILEGE removes every privilege but SeChangeNotifyPrivilege.
- TokenRestrictedSids and TokenHasRestrictions are answered.

Integrity levels
- TokenIntegrityLevel is read from the token's mandatory label and can be
  lowered; raising it is refused (it needs SeRelabelPrivilege).

AppContainer (lowbox) tokens
- NtCreateLowBoxToken / CreateAppContainerToken make a copy of a token that
  carries a package SID and capabilities, at low integrity, whose default
  DACL also grants the package. An access check must then also be granted
  by the package SID, one of its capabilities or ALL APPLICATION PACKAGES.
- CreateProcess with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_SECURITY_CAPABILITIES starts the
  process with such a token.
- TokenIsAppContainer, TokenAppContainerSid, TokenCapabilities and
  TokenAppContainerNumber are answered.
- Not done: a package's own named-object directory (its named objects live
  with everyone else's), and less-privileged AppContainers (LPAC) are
  treated as ordinary AppContainers, so ALL APPLICATION PACKAGES grants to
  them too.

Also: TokenSecurityAttributes (class 39) answers an empty list rather than
failing, and UpdateProcThreadAttribute accepts the documented attributes
Chromium sets (mitigation, child-process, desktop-app and all-application-
packages policies, component filter, protection level, group affinity,
preferred node, optional XState features); CreateProcess still ignores the
policies.

The access check's owner-rights and DACL walk moved into a helper so it can
run with each set of SIDs; its behaviour for an ordinary token is
unchanged. New requests: get_token_label, set_token_label,
create_lowbox_token, get_token_appcontainer; filter_token and
get_token_groups gained fields (protocol regenerated with make_requests --
every Unix module must be rebuilt, since the request numbers move).

